A forwarded signal handler must bracket itself, and every signal is forwarded.

interrupt_enter/interrupt_exit tell the daemon a handler is running. Two things were wrong.

The pair was skipped whenever in-guest IPC was on, justified by sigexc having no daemon Mach wait to
clear. But interrupt_enter also asks the daemon to hold replies while the handler runs, and without
it a signal arriving mid-call lets the handler's own calls consume the reply the interrupted call
was waiting for. The two streams then sit one out of step for the rest of the process's life.

And the bracketed wrapper was installed for SIGCHLD and SIGTTOU only. Every other signal, SIGWINCH
among them, went straight to the bare handler with no bracket at all, so the protection that did
exist covered two signals out of thirty-one.

iTerm2 finds it: it sets the pty window size after laying out its window, so SIGWINCH reaches login
in the middle of mach_reply_port. Standalone login never gets one, which is why it execs a shell
there and hangs here. The trace showed 110 requests against 111 replies with no interrupt_enter
anywhere.

THIS IS NOT THE WHOLE FIX AND LOGIN STILL HANGS. ciderd says why in its own comment on
interrupt_enter: "the getcontext dance for a signal that interrupts a BLOCKED daemon call, running
that call's continuation nested and deferring its reply, is a later refinement". That refinement is
the missing half, and it is exactly the case here.

Regression: Swift Publisher 344 distinct colours, iA Writer 357, MoneyMoney 823, LibreOffice 175,
zero faults in all four.

--- a/darling/src/libsystem_kernel/emulation/src/xnu_syscall/bsd/impl/signal/sigaction.c
+++ b/darling/src/libsystem_kernel/emulation/src/xnu_syscall/bsd/impl/signal/sigaction.c
@@ -136,7 +136,10 @@
 		else*/ if (nsa->sa_sigaction != SIG_DFL && nsa->sa_sigaction != SIG_IGN
 				&& nsa->sa_sigaction != SIG_ERR)
 		{
-			sa.sa_sigaction = &handler_linux_to_bsd;
+			/* EVERY forwarded signal needs the interrupt bracket, not just the two that had it.
+			 * SIGCHLD and SIGTTOU got the wrapper and the rest went straight to the handler, so a
+			 * SIGWINCH arriving mid-call left the request and reply streams one out of step. */
+			sa.sa_sigaction = &handler_linux_to_bsd_wrapper;
 		}
 		else
 			sa.sa_sigaction = (linux_sig_handler*) nsa->sa_sigaction;
@@ -154,7 +157,7 @@
 
 	if (osa != NULL)
 	{
-		if (olsa.sa_sigaction == handler_linux_to_bsd)
+		if (olsa.sa_sigaction == handler_linux_to_bsd_wrapper)
 			osa->sa_sigaction = sig_handlers[linux_signum];
 		else // values such as SIG_DFL
 			osa->sa_sigaction = (bsd_sig_handler*) olsa.sa_sigaction;
@@ -175,14 +178,21 @@
 #endif
 
 static void handler_linux_to_bsd_wrapper(int linux_signum, struct linux_siginfo* info, void* ctxt) {
-	// Self-contained (flag-on): interrupt is a no-op -- sigexc_enter has no ciderd Mach wait to clear,
-	// and its user_state is read only by thread_get_state, which a forwarded handler never calls.
-	int inguest = __inguest_ipc_on();
-	if (!inguest)
-		dserver_rpc_interrupt_enter();
+	/*
+	 * THE PAIR IS NOT ONLY ABOUT sigexc, WHICH IS WHY IT USED TO BE SKIPPED WITH IN-GUEST IPC ON.
+	 *
+	 * interrupt_enter also tells the daemon to hold replies while a handler runs. Without it, a
+	 * signal delivered while a call is waiting lets the handler's own calls consume the reply the
+	 * interrupted call was waiting for: the request and reply streams go one out of step and stay
+	 * that way, so a later call waits for a reply that was already taken.
+	 *
+	 * iTerm2 sets the pty window size after laying out its window, SIGWINCH reaches login in the
+	 * middle of mach_reply_port, and login then waits forever and never execs the shell. The trace
+	 * shows it exactly: 110 requests, 111 replies, no interrupt_enter anywhere.
+	 */
+	dserver_rpc_interrupt_enter();
 	handler_linux_to_bsd(linux_signum, info, ctxt);
-	if (!inguest)
-		dserver_rpc_interrupt_exit();
+	dserver_rpc_interrupt_exit();
 };
 
